the United States currently faces many  long-term policy challenges and each of  them is characterized by the common  denominator that we have to make hard  choices today in order to ensure  benefits for the future and that we're  not doing a very good job of addressing  these challenges one that's been in the  news a lot recently is climate change  where the increasing scientific evidence  is mounting suggesting that climate  change is going to have devastating  impacts on the American economy and the  way we live our lives yet we see very  little initiative to invest in costs  today in order to mitigate these future  damages similarly another area where  climate change comes into play is  disaster management we've recently seen  examples of terrible natural disasters  such as Hurricane Katrina and hurricane  sandy that have devastated coastal  regions of the United States and in each  of these cases the local governments and  the national governments did not take  ex-ante measures in order to mitigate  the disasters but rather just came in  after that they had caused their damage  outside of the domain of climate we also  see cases of public pensions in debt  where we have challenges facing the  long-term economic security and  financial health of the United States  with respect to the debt we have a lot  of entitlement obligations such as  Social Security and Medicare that are  not being addressed given the changing  demographics and aging nature of the  American population and governors  throughout the United States of both  political parties have basically  promised a great deal of pensions to  public sector employees as opposed to  increasing taxes in wages today and  there really is no good way to pay for  these things and each of these cases we  don't see very much mobilization on the  part of the political class to address  these long-term policymaking challenges  so one question that arises is who's to  blame for our failure to address these  long-term policymaking challenges now  the media always likes to denigrate the  political class and political elites as  it's very easy to say that politicians  are not doing a good job and the process  in Washington is broken however our  research has suggested that the American  voter is actually to blame for a lot of  these problems in the way that voters  incentivize politicians so I'll give you  two examples so one of our research  projects collected data on all the  disasters that had taken place since  1988 along with election returns and  government policy making in the disaster  domain and what we found is that voters  do a pretty good job of rewarding  politicians for disaster relief spending  but do a very poor job for rewarding  them for disaster preparation spending  and you can see how these electoral  patterns create bad incentives for  politicians to essentially not spend  money before a disaster takes place to  undergo efficient mechanisms in order to  mitigate risk but rather to simply send  in a very expensive cavalry after the  disaster has taken place and then get  the credit for solving the problem  another example we found is that voters  oftentimes they're not very forward  looking towards the future but instead  are really subject to the whims at the  moment so we found that the local  college football game outcomes a few  days before the election significantly  influenced how well the incumbents do  because they represent shocks to  well-being in mood so a win versus a  loss by the local college football team  increased an incumbents vote share by  about 1.6 percentage points suggesting  that voters are overly influenced by  their mood and not doing all that they  need to do to be effective Democratic  citizens  then the question arises of what we can  actually do to address this problem and  failure of long-term policy making one  issue is that the United States is  characterized by short terms at least  compared to many other advanced  democracies on the planet now the  problem with short terms is that even  though they increase accountability  between representatives and constituents  they make politicians very short-sighted  the analogy would be a CEO who's so  concerned with quarterly earnings that  they forget about long-term health of  their shareholders employees and other  key stakeholder groups now it's not  going to be easy to lengthen terms in  the United States so what else can we do  well the media I think can do a lot to  educate voters about what's important to  incentivize politicians properly in the  case of disasters we rarely see the  media for example cover preventive  solutions to disasters or actions that  the government is taken to mitigate ex  ante risk but we oftentimes see young  great images of governors and presidents  kind of flying to disaster prone areas  and covering all the relief that gets  done and it's this kind of media  coverage that leads to bad attributions  and prevents the electorate from holding  the government accountable for the right  things I think one other problem in the  United States has been a recent  phenomenon is that the campaign cycles  are just too long we are in this period  of the permanent campaign so the 2014  midterm elections haven't even happened  yet yet the media is devoting extensive  coverage to the 2016 presidential  election and the problem with this is  that politicians know they have only a  few months to get policy done before  they have to go and start dealing with  the issue of the election once again and  this again makes people short term  minded if they're going to be evaluated  very soon after they come into office so  basically if the media focus less on the  next campaign and more on the policy  challenges facing the country then  voters might do a better job  you
 

